WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:03.336
Bertha's family is trying
desperately to break in.

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:05.037
She's very socially ambitious,

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:08.274
and what that means is that she
has to sort of break the code.

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:10.042 line:0%
♪ (LIVELY
ORCHESTRAL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:10.075 --> 00:00:11.511
This sort of stunt
does not impress

00:00:11.544 --> 00:00:13.012
the people you want to win over.

00:00:13.045 --> 00:00:16.015
Mrs. Morris, this sort of stunt
impresses everyone.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:17.350
CARRIE COON:<i> She is new money.</i>

00:00:17.383 --> 00:00:19.352
<i> She's part of this
 new wave of industrialists,</i>

00:00:19.385 --> 00:00:22.421
<i> so her husband has gotten his
hand into the railroad industry,</i>

00:00:22.454 --> 00:00:24.356
<i> and they've made a fortune
 very rapidly.</i>

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:26.392
<i> But, of course,
 the old money families</i>

00:00:26.426 --> 00:00:27.727
<i> are trying to protect
 their position</i>

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:29.562
<i> as the American aristocracy.</i>

00:00:29.595 --> 00:00:32.231
I don't know which is worse,
the noise of the builders

00:00:32.265 --> 00:00:34.734
or the chance of running
into her in the street.

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:38.271
COON:<i> Bertha has a neighbor
 across the street,</i>

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:40.707
Agnes van Rhijn, and her sister
living in a brownstone.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.107
<i> They're part
 of the old money families.</i>

00:00:42.141 --> 00:00:43.876
<i> They're sort of
 more socially conservative.</i>

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.111
They're not flashy
with their wealth.

00:00:45.144 --> 00:00:46.913
<i>And Bertha Russell has moved in
 and built</i>

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:50.283
<i> this palatial residence right
 across the street from them,</i>

00:00:50.316 --> 00:00:51.884
<i> and they won't look her
 in the eye.</i>

00:00:51.917 --> 00:00:54.554
And so part of the arc
of the season for her

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:56.722
<i> is trying to figure out
 how to position herself</i>

00:00:56.755 --> 00:01:00.626
<i> so that Agnes van Rhijn has
to acknowledge that she exists.</i>

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.762
She becomes a real foil
for Bertha.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:04.397
She represents everything
Bertha isn't.

00:01:05.765 --> 00:01:08.267
<i> I was also really drawn
 to playing Bertha because</i>

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:11.904
<i> I found the marriage between
 George and Bertha Russell
 really compelling.</i>

00:01:11.938 --> 00:01:14.240
It struck me
as shockingly egalitarian

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:15.642
for the time that this is set.

00:01:15.675 --> 00:01:18.077
<i> I love that they're really
 in this together.</i>

00:01:18.111 --> 00:01:21.414
There are moments, my dear,
when you are quite marvelous.

00:01:21.447 --> 00:01:23.849
Useless, each without the other.

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.985
COON:<i> You get the sense that
 they're very compatible,</i>

00:01:26.018 --> 00:01:28.588
<i> and they're hungry
 for the next step.</i>

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:29.922
I just found
that really thrilling.

00:01:30.289 --> 00:01:32.358
What I am planning may take
a great many dollars, my dear.

00:01:32.424 --> 00:01:33.525
We've made one fortune together.

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:35.528
If needs be, we'll make
and spend another.

00:01:37.463 --> 00:01:40.032
COON:<i> I also think it's a really
 interesting time to consider</i>

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:42.502
how we feel about how
these billionaires are living,

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:44.436
<i> and the way
 that wealth is flaunted</i>

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:46.538
<i>in spite of tremendous hardship.</i>

00:01:46.605 --> 00:01:48.608
And, of course, in addition
to that, all the dynamics

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:53.012
<i>of love and family and marriage,
 and people wanting to fit in,</i>

00:01:53.045 --> 00:01:55.848
and wanting to know
what their place is.

00:01:55.881 --> 00:01:57.717
BERTHA RUSSELL:
<i> We're finally getting
 to where we belong.</i>

00:02:00.119 --> 00:02:01.955
MORGAN SPECTOR:<i> George is
a fist in a velvet glove, right?</i>

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:05.458
He is a late 19th century
industrialist.

00:02:05.491 --> 00:02:06.492
<i> A robber baron.</i>

00:02:06.526 --> 00:02:07.994
What will you do
if he won't play ball?

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:11.330
Build another line,
right alongside his.

00:02:11.364 --> 00:02:13.466
He is based a little bit
on Jay Gould.

00:02:13.499 --> 00:02:15.969
<i> Gould was really vilified
 in the papers of the time.</i>

00:02:16.002 --> 00:02:18.805
<i> He was someone who became
 a kind of living embodiment of</i>

00:02:18.838 --> 00:02:21.341
all of the inequality
of the era.

00:02:21.374 --> 00:02:23.176
Russell has more money
than God.

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:26.045
SPECTOR:<i> He has such
 insane levels of power.</i>

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:29.481
And sometimes he uses them
on really vulnerable people,

00:02:29.515 --> 00:02:31.084
<i>and just crushes them like ants.</i>

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:32.485
You bastard.

00:02:32.518 --> 00:02:34.820
I may be a bastard,
Mr. Thorburn,

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:36.422
but you are a fool.

00:02:36.488 --> 00:02:39.092
George, at home,
kind of gets his ass kicked.

00:02:39.158 --> 00:02:40.159
I have a favor to ask.

00:02:40.192 --> 00:02:41.360
You mean you want something,

00:02:41.393 --> 00:02:42.828
and you haven't asked
your mother.

00:02:42.862 --> 00:02:45.064
He is most fundamentally
a family man.

00:02:45.097 --> 00:02:48.901
<i> I mean, a person
 who's really driven
 by his love for his children.</i>

00:02:48.934 --> 00:02:51.837
<i> There's a sense of George
 holding his power in reserve.</i>

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:54.741
I said I had no objection, but
she had to get your permission.

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.543
I will decide who comes
to dinner in this house.

00:02:57.576 --> 00:03:00.913
George's relationship
with Bertha is, for me,
at the heart of the show.

00:03:00.947 --> 00:03:02.515
I just like you to be happy.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.918
And I know my loving you
is not enough.

00:03:05.952 --> 00:03:07.253
It's almost enough.

00:03:07.286 --> 00:03:09.722
She has that kind of desire
for power.

00:03:09.755 --> 00:03:12.692
But because of the era
in which she lived,

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.727
<i> the way that that gets
 to express itself</i>

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.862
is in this quest
for social status.

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:20.365
<i> And I think George
 sort of understands
 this is what she has,</i>

00:03:20.399 --> 00:03:21.734
and so he facilitates it,

00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:22.935
and fights for it
on her behalf,

00:03:23.736 --> 00:03:25.637
<i> because she cares about it
 and so he cares about.</i>

00:03:25.704 --> 00:03:29.108
You and Mrs. Morris have
snubbed and belittled my wife.

00:03:29.141 --> 00:03:31.311
How could I allow that
to go unpunished?

00:03:31.377 --> 00:03:32.945
There's something very romantic
about that.

00:03:33.112 --> 00:03:34.380
<i> It's just fascinating.</i>

00:03:34.813 --> 00:03:37.883
There's an aspect of George
where he gives his own fury

00:03:37.916 --> 00:03:39.585
free reign sometimes.

00:03:39.618 --> 00:03:40.753
I'm afraid you must face
the music.

00:03:41.454 --> 00:03:43.890
It's why the sort of duality of
being this very warm family man,

00:03:43.923 --> 00:03:45.291
<i> but in business,</i>

00:03:45.324 --> 00:03:48.127
<i> being just utterly,
 almost inhuman,</i>

00:03:48.427 --> 00:03:49.595
is fascinating.

00:03:50.763 --> 00:03:52.498
HARRY RICHARDSON:
<i> Larry was just such a kind</i>

00:03:52.564 --> 00:03:54.167
and excitable young man.

00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.335
I really related
to his drive

00:03:56.401 --> 00:03:58.804
to try to figure out
where he sits in everything.

00:03:58.838 --> 00:04:00.906
And what sort of man
he's going to be.

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:02.675
♪ (CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:04:03.076 --> 00:04:06.912
Larry is the young, happy,
go-lucky son

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:08.848
of George and Bertha Russell.

00:04:08.915 --> 00:04:13.419
He's a recent Harvard grad,
and he comes back from school

00:04:13.452 --> 00:04:16.956
<i> to try and figure out
 where his place is in society,</i>

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.657
and in the world,
and in his family.

00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:21.260
<i> Larry's relationship
 with his parents</i>

00:04:21.293 --> 00:04:23.362
<i> is pretty cheeky and fun.</i>

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:26.632
He loves both of them very much.
He really adores his mother

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:29.201
<i> and he'll wind her up
 and make fun</i>

00:04:29.268 --> 00:04:31.471
<i> of the things that she holds
 very dear.</i>

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:33.105
Well, obviously,
my mother couldn't live

00:04:33.138 --> 00:04:34.307
in a house without a ballroom.

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.542
(CHUCKLES) You may laugh,
but as my mother

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:40.012
never tires of pointing out,
our future success in New York

00:04:40.045 --> 00:04:41.980
depends entirely
on this important approval

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:43.216
of Mrs. Astor.

00:04:44.717 --> 00:04:46.819
HARRY:<i> Larry's relationship
 with Gladys</i>

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:47.787
<i> is really sweet.</i>

00:04:48.454 --> 00:04:50.723
They really are best friends,
and he looks after her

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.325
<i> and really sticks up for her
 when she needs someone</i>

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:56.228
in her corner to fight
against her controlling mom.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:57.696
She's not a child anymore.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:58.965
And you shouldn't treat her
as one.

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:00.466
She's a child until I say.

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:05.070
He's a little bit
of a disruptor

00:05:05.137 --> 00:05:06.172
within his family.

00:05:06.706 --> 00:05:09.041
He's obviously got all
of these societal expectations

00:05:09.074 --> 00:05:11.644
<i> of who he's meant to be,
 and what job he's meant</i>

00:05:11.678 --> 00:05:13.579
<i> to undertake,
 and who he's meant</i>

00:05:13.645 --> 00:05:15.080
<i> to be partnered with.</i>

00:05:15.147 --> 00:05:18.051
And he has a little bit of cheek
and starts to reject

00:05:18.084 --> 00:05:20.485
and question some of the things
that are expected of him.

00:05:20.519 --> 00:05:21.587
I should get going,
I'm catching

00:05:21.654 --> 00:05:23.423
the 11 o'clock train
to Rhode Island.

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:24.757
But you've only just got home.

00:05:24.824 --> 00:05:26.559
(CHUCKLES) Is that
what this is?

00:05:28.594 --> 00:05:30.763
Gladys has a bit
of a rebellious nature,

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:31.764
just a hint of it.

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:33.165
She doesn't always want
to conform

00:05:33.198 --> 00:05:34.434
to the rules of high society.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:37.102
♪ (ORCHESTRAL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:05:37.703 --> 00:05:39.272
Gladys is the daughter

00:05:39.338 --> 00:05:42.074
of the richest new money family,
the Russells.

00:05:42.107 --> 00:05:44.744
She, like any teenager
on the brink of adulthood,

00:05:45.244 --> 00:05:47.680
<i>is craving her own independence</i>

00:05:47.713 --> 00:05:50.249
<i> and the chance to experience
 the world.</i>

00:05:50.849 --> 00:05:52.184
SPEAKER:
I trust you'll take our part.

00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:53.285
I certainly intend to.

00:05:53.952 --> 00:05:56.222
My daughter isn't out yet.
She's only here to accompany me.

00:05:57.189 --> 00:05:59.091
But she's stuck.
She's stuck with the mother

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:00.893
who has so much control
over her.

00:06:00.926 --> 00:06:03.596
<i> She's stuck in a society
 where young women</i>

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:05.230
<i> are not allowed to have
 their own identities</i>

00:06:05.264 --> 00:06:07.533
or enjoy a sense of freedom
without a husband.

00:06:07.566 --> 00:06:09.368
How strange these rules are.

00:06:09.635 --> 00:06:10.803
Why shouldn't you go out
to dinner

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:11.804
with your brother and a friend?

00:06:12.237 --> 00:06:14.239
-What could be more normal?
-Not to my mother.

00:06:14.273 --> 00:06:16.475
There's a mutual respect
between Gladys

00:06:16.541 --> 00:06:17.977
and her brother Larry.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.613
<i> They both bond
 over their struggle</i>

00:06:20.646 --> 00:06:23.416
with the unfair expectations
of their parents.

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.717
I suppose Mother's made
another quarrel.

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:27.486
I'm afraid I can't stay either.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:30.490
I just wanted to look in to pay
my respects to your parents.

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.725
-I hope we can meet again.
-I'm sure we will.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:35.561
For Gladys, she enjoys
the fancy dresses

00:06:35.595 --> 00:06:38.163
<i> and the French chef
 and the butlers and the maids</i>

00:06:38.230 --> 00:06:40.900
<i>and all that, but she feels like
 she could give all that up</i>

00:06:40.933 --> 00:06:44.070
just to be happy. She doesn't
have that same desire

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:47.239
<i> or desperation to be accepted
 into high society</i>

00:06:47.273 --> 00:06:48.774
the way that her mother,
Bertha, does.

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:51.277
I gather we're going to christen
the new house.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.478
Just a few people calling in,
but I think it's time.

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:54.513
How many are coming?

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:56.949
It's hard to say as they
don't accept in a formal way,

00:06:56.982 --> 00:06:58.484
but I think if we cater
for 200,

00:06:58.751 --> 00:07:00.520
-it's probably safe.
-Wow!

00:07:00.586 --> 00:07:03.089
In this world of fancy dinners
and ball gowns

00:07:03.122 --> 00:07:05.491
and etiquette classes,
Gladys can sometimes feel like

00:07:05.658 --> 00:07:06.825
<i> more of a doll than a human</i>

00:07:07.359 --> 00:07:09.795
and feels like
she's suffocating sometimes,

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:11.197
and it's all for the sake
of a future

00:07:11.263 --> 00:07:12.298
that's not even hers.

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:14.166
Mother, may I present
Mr. van Rhijn?

00:07:14.666 --> 00:07:15.601
How do you know my daughter,
sir?

00:07:16.034 --> 00:07:18.837
Well, I don't. Not really.
But I want to very much.

00:07:19.104 --> 00:07:20.806
We cannot always have
what we want.

00:07:22.508 --> 00:07:24.476
JACK GILPIN:
<i> Church is the kind of person
 when he sees something</i>

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:26.278
<i> that's not happening right,
 he goes to it.</i>

00:07:26.311 --> 00:07:27.346
He doesn't walk past it.

00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:31.783
Church relates differently
to Mr. and Mrs. Russell each.

00:07:32.217 --> 00:07:33.485
GEORGE RUSSELL:
Is Mrs. Russell at home?

00:07:33.519 --> 00:07:35.655
She's in the drawing room, sir,
with Mrs. Morris.

00:07:35.688 --> 00:07:36.956
Is she, indeed?

00:07:37.623 --> 00:07:38.724
Well, I won't disturb them.

00:07:39.225 --> 00:07:40.793
GILPIN:<i> I love my relationship
 with Mr. Russell.</i>

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:42.294
We don't have much to say
to each other,

00:07:43.028 --> 00:07:45.164
but when he has something
to say to me, I know
it's gonna be of substance,

00:07:45.698 --> 00:07:46.798
and I know he values my advice.

00:07:47.733 --> 00:07:49.569
It's thought of
as a male-dominated culture,

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:52.805
<i> which it was,
 but that was in one area.</i>

00:07:53.172 --> 00:07:55.741
<i> In the realm of the home
 and the household,</i>

00:07:55.808 --> 00:07:57.809
women called the shots.

00:07:57.843 --> 00:07:59.578
<i>The household is an enterprise,</i>

00:07:59.645 --> 00:08:01.980
<i> which can be more successful
 and less successful,</i>

00:08:02.014 --> 00:08:04.049
and Church has a big investment

00:08:04.082 --> 00:08:05.918
in the success
of that enterprise

00:08:05.984 --> 00:08:07.219
<i> as Mrs. Russell does.</i>

00:08:07.252 --> 00:08:09.354
And is everything ready
for Mr. Larry's return?

00:08:09.388 --> 00:08:11.824
I believe so, madam, but I will
check with Mrs. Bruce.

00:08:11.857 --> 00:08:12.858
Thank you, Church.

00:08:12.892 --> 00:08:16.028
Mrs. Russell wants
very passionately

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:18.698
to establish a beachhead
in New York society.

00:08:19.065 --> 00:08:21.099
<i> I want to help her do that.</i>

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.702
He is the butler.
It's his job to do this,

00:08:23.735 --> 00:08:25.004
but he would do it anyway.

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:26.405
<i> In this kind of household,</i>

00:08:26.439 --> 00:08:29.107
there are three principal heads
of the staff.

00:08:29.174 --> 00:08:31.677
<i> There's the butler, the cook,
 and the housekeeper.</i>

00:08:32.044 --> 00:08:33.245
<i> The butler has overall
 responsibility</i>

00:08:33.278 --> 00:08:34.914
<i> for the management
 of the household.</i>

00:08:34.947 --> 00:08:35.948
We live together,

00:08:36.381 --> 00:08:39.952
so it's not like
a normal business relationship.

00:08:40.018 --> 00:08:41.186
We have to get along.

00:08:41.219 --> 00:08:42.554
This house is a folly.

00:08:42.587 --> 00:08:45.057
With one exception
in this staff,

00:08:45.090 --> 00:08:46.892
I have a great relationship
with them.

00:08:46.926 --> 00:08:49.895
Well, Miss Turner, I hope
you can overcome your distaste

00:08:49.929 --> 00:08:51.363
and give the mistress
a helping hand

00:08:51.397 --> 00:08:52.931
with her clothes
for the evening.

00:08:52.965 --> 00:08:54.066
GILPIN:
<i> Turner, the ladies' maid,</i>

00:08:54.099 --> 00:08:55.901
we have
kind of a hostile relationship.

00:08:56.568 --> 00:08:58.303
<i> I like Church.
 He has a lot of pride,</i>

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:00.706
professional pride,
and I admire that a lot.

